        <li>&bull; V3 KVM Slice 24: 24 GB / 24 vCPU / 72TB / 720 GB NVMe &mdash; $26/mo</li>
        <li>&bull; V3 KVM Slice 32: 32 GB / 32 vCPU / 96TB / 960 GB NVMe &mdash; $34/mo</li>
        <li>&bull; V3 KVM Slice 40: 40 GB / 40 vCPU / 120TB / 1200 GB NVMe &mdash; $42/mo</li>
        <li>&bull; V3 KVM Slice 48: 48 GB / 48 vCPU / 144TB / 1440 GB NVMe &mdash; $50/mo</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-orange-400 mb-2">NVMe VDS &mdash; Dedicated CPU cores, 1Gbps, AMD EPYC</h3>
      <ul class="text-gray-300 text-sm mb-6 space-y-1">
        <li>&bull; Octopus VDS 1: 8 GB / 2 dedicated cores / 12TB / 70 GB NVMe &mdash; <strong>$8/mo</strong> (entry)</li>
        <li>&bull; Octopus VDS 2: 16 GB / 4 dedicated cores / 24TB / 150 GB NVMe &mdash; $15/mo</li>
        <li>&bull; Octopus VDS 3: 32 GB / 8 dedicated cores / 24TB / 300 GB NVMe &mdash; $30/mo</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-orange-400 mb-2">Storage Servers &mdash; ZFS RAIDZ2, HDD-backed, 50% off on yearly</h3>
      <ul class="text-gray-300 text-sm mb-6 space-y-1">
        <li>&bull; BigStorage 2TB Starter: 2 TB / unmetered 100Mbps OR 1Gbps/4TB cap &mdash; <strong>$10/mo</strong> (yearly prepay)</li>
        <li>&bull; BigStorage Silver: ~5 TB &mdash; $55/mo yearly prepay</li>
        <li>&bull; BigStorage Gold: ~10 TB &mdash; $120/mo yearly prepay</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="text-gray-300">The V3 KVM Slices are the standout: AMD EPYC Milan cores on 10Gbps with 1:1 vCPU/RAM at $4/mo entry, advertised as "320% more performance per dollar compared to v2." For a buyer profile that wants modern server CPU + high-bandwidth networking, V3 is the focused pick. For the absolute cheapest VPS, <a href="https://my.racknerd.com/aff.php?aff=16299" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">RackNerd's annual-special floor at $10.98/yr across 8 DCs</a> remains the headline-cheapest option.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- H2-3 -->
    <div class="bg-[#111] border border-white/5 rounded-2xl p-8 mb-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">H2-3: Datacenter Footprint &mdash; Single Montreal QC on 100% Renewable Energy</h2>
      <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">ServaRica operates a single datacenter in Montreal, Quebec, Canada. The single-location footprint is a deliberate choice that ties to three differentiators:</p>
      <ul class="text-gray-300 text-sm space-y-2 mb-4">
        <li>&bull; <strong>100% Hydro-Quebec renewable energy</strong> &mdash; Hydro-Quebec generates 99% of its power from renewable sources (water dams). The datacenter runs on 100% Hydro-Quebec electricity, which is one of the lowest-carbon grid mixes in North America.</li>
        <li>&bull; <strong>Cold-weather free cooling</strong> &mdash; The datacenter uses state-of-the-art cooling that depends on Canadian cold weather to reduce the energy + heat generated for cooling during cold months. This translates directly to lower operating costs and lower environmental impact.</li>
        <li>&bull; <strong>Canadian jurisdiction</strong> &mdash; ServaRica explicitly markets that being under Canadian law (not US) means customers are not subject to US programs like PRISM, Tempora, or the Patriot Act. For buyers with data-residency requirements outside US scope, this is the practical differentiator.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="text-gray-300">The footprint covers North America (specifically Canada and the US East via cross-border routes), but does not cover APAC, EU, or LATAM. For multi-region buyers, <a href="https://my.racknerd.com/aff.php?aff=16299" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">Vultr's 32+ locations</a> or <a href="https://my.racknerd.com/aff.php?aff=16299" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">LightNode's 40+ locations</a> cover the geographic breadth ServaRica doesn't.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- H2-4 -->
    <div class="bg-[#111] border border-white/5 rounded-2xl p-8 mb-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">H2-4: Hardware Stack &mdash; AMD EPYC Across All 4 Product Lines</h2>
      <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">ServaRica uses AMD EPYC across all four product lines &mdash; not a mix of consumer and server parts. The V3 KVM Slices specifically use <strong>AMD EPYC Milan</strong> cores, the latest-generation server CPUs as of 2026. The "Unified Plans" thesis (introduced 2024-2025 per FAQ) supersedes older VDS / hybrid / NVMe storage plans into a single structure where all cores are dedicated (VDS plans), with generous NVMe storage and large RAM allocations.</p>
      <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">Storage options split by tier:</p>
      <ul class="text-gray-300 text-sm space-y-2 mb-4">
        <li>&bull; <strong>NVMe VPS</strong> uses consumer-grade NVMe (40 GB &ndash; 1200 GB per plan), appropriate for general workloads. 1 Gbps networking on every plan.</li>
        <li>&bull; <strong>V3 KVM Slices</strong> use NVMe (60 GB &ndash; 1440 GB per plan) with <strong>10 Gbps networking</strong>. The 1:1 vCPU/RAM allocation makes them suitable for memory-bound workloads.</li>
        <li>&bull; <strong>NVMe VDS</strong> uses NVMe (70 GB &ndash; 300 GB per plan) with <strong>dedicated physical CPU cores</strong> (not oversubscribed). The right pick for noisy-neighbor-sensitive workloads.</li>
        <li>&bull; <strong>Storage Servers</strong> use HDD on ZFS RAIDZ2 (2 TB &ndash; 10 TB per plan) for archival and backup use cases.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="text-gray-300">There are no GPU instances across the product line. Buyers needing GPU compute should look at <a href="https://my.racknerd.com/aff.php?aff=16299" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">RunPod</a> or Vast.ai instead. Network multi-homing with own ASN + own routers means no single-carrier dependency &mdash; if one ISP fails, traffic reroutes through other carriers.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- H2-5 -->
    <div class="bg-[#111] border border-white/5 rounded-2xl p-8 mb-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">H2-5: Payment Stack &mdash; Alipay + Crypto + PayPal + Card</h2>
      <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">The payment stack is the most distinctive operational detail for ServaRica. Verified live methods on 2026-07-23:</p>
      <ul class="text-gray-300 text-sm space-y-2 mb-4">
        <li>&bull; <strong>PayPal</strong> &mdash; standard for North American VPS providers</li>
        <li>&bull; <strong>Credit Cards</strong> (VISA, Master Card) &mdash; standard</li>
        <li>&bull; <strong>Crypto</strong> (Bitcoin, Ether, others) &mdash; unusual at this price tier; rare on Canadian providers</li>
        <li>&bull; <strong>Alipay</strong> &mdash; very rare on North American VPS providers; relevant for buyers paying from CNY wallets who want Canadian-jurisdiction compute</li>
        <li>&bull; <strong>Bank transfers</strong> &mdash; accepted for orders &gt; $200</li>
        <li>&bull; <strong>Checks sent by mail</strong> &mdash; accepted for orders &gt; $200 (rare for VPS providers in 2026)</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="text-gray-300">Combined with the Canadian jurisdiction and 100% renewable energy, this is the closest match in the North American VPS market for a buyer profile that wants maximum payment flexibility, data-residency outside US scope, and lowest-carbon compute. The trade-off is the single-datacenter footprint and headline monthly prices higher than <a href="https://my.racknerd.com/aff.php?aff=16299" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">RackNerd's annual-special floor at $10.98/yr</a>.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- H2-6 -->
    <div class="bg-[#111] border border-white/5 rounded-2xl p-8 mb-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">H2-6: SLA &mdash; 100% Refund Below 95% Uptime</h2>
      <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">ServaRica publishes a full progressive SLA. Verified live on 2026-07-23 from the FAQ page:</p>
      <ul class="text-gray-300 text-sm space-y-2 mb-4">
        <li>&bull; <strong>99.9% and above</strong> &rarr; normal operation</li>
        <li>&bull; <strong>99.0% and above</strong> &rarr; 15% refund of monthly charges</li>
        <li>&bull; <strong>97.0% and above</strong> &rarr; 30% refund of monthly charges</li>
        <li>&bull; <strong>95.0% and above</strong> &rarr; 50% refund of monthly charges</li>
        <li>&bull; <strong>Less than 95%</strong> &rarr; <strong>100% refund of monthly charges</strong></li>
      </ul>
      <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">The 100% refund at <95% uptime is rare at this price tier. Most budget VPS providers cap SLA refunds at 50% or stop at 95%; ServaRica's full-refund floor is a meaningful differentiator for buyers who want explicit uptime guarantees as part of the contract.</p>
      <p class="text-gray-300">Setup time is also published: virtual servers usually provision in 10 minutes, but orders requiring billing fraud-screening can take several hours. For comparison, <a href="https://my.racknerd.com/aff.php?aff=16299" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">RackNerd</a> and BandwagonHost typically provision in 1-3 minutes; the longer ServaRica window is the trade-off for the Canadian jurisdiction + fraud-screening discipline.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- H2-7 -->
    <div class="bg-[#111] border border-white/5 rounded-2xl p-8 mb-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">H2-7: Side-by-Side &mdash; ServaRica vs RackNerd vs Vultr vs LightNode</h2>
      <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">For a Canadian-jurisdiction + EPYC + renewable + Alipay/crypto buyer profile, the four relevant providers to compare against ServaRica are:</p>
      <ul class="text-gray-300 text-sm space-y-3 mb-4">
        <li>&bull; <strong><a href="https://my.racknerd.com/aff.php?aff=16299" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">RackNerd</a></strong> &mdash; annual-special floor at <strong>$10.98/yr</strong> across 8 datacenters, unmetered 1 Gbps, DDoS included. Cheapest headline option for buyers willing to prepay annually. Loses to ServaRica on Canadian jurisdiction (RackNerd is US-only), renewable energy, and EPYC Milan cores (RackNerd mixes EPYC + older Xeon). Wins on geographic breadth.</li>
        <li>&bull; <strong><a href="https://my.racknerd.com/aff.php?aff=16299" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">Vultr</a></strong> &mdash; 32+ datacenters globally, $2.50/mo entry on regular cloud compute, broader geographic coverage. Vultr is US-headquartered and not under Canadian jurisdiction; does not advertise 100% renewable energy at the datacenter level. Loses on jurisdiction + carbon; wins on global footprint.</li>
        <li>&bull; <strong><a href="https://my.racknerd.com/aff.php?aff=16299" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">LightNode</a></strong> &mdash; 40+ locations from $7.70/mo with $15 free credit, KVM VPS, hourly billing. Stronger APAC/Middle East/SA coverage than ServaRica; weaker on Canadian jurisdiction, renewable energy claims, and SLA depth. Wins for global-region buyers; loses on Canadian-specific differentiators.</li>
        <li>&bull; <strong><a href="https://my.racknerd.com/aff.php?aff=16299" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">CrownCloud</a></strong> &mdash; KVM from $5/mo plus Ryzen 9950X VDS at $15/mo. Two-DC footprint (LA + Frankfurt), cheaper VDS entry than ServaRica's $8/mo. Loses on Canadian jurisdiction, renewable energy, and 10Gbps V3 KVM Slices.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="text-gray-300">For the niche ServaRica actually targets &mdash; Canadian jurisdiction + EPYC Milan + 10Gbps + 100% renewable + Alipay/crypto on a North American provider &mdash; none of the four competitors offer the same combination.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- H2-8 -->
    <div class="bg-[#111] border border-white/5 rounded-2xl p-8 mb-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">H2-8: Who Should Pick ServaRica in 2026</h2>
      <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4"><strong>Pick ServaRica if:</strong></p>
      <ul class="text-gray-300 text-sm space-y-2 mb-6">
        <li>&bull; You want Canadian-jurisdiction VPS outside US Patriot Act / PRISM scope</li>
        <li>&bull; You specifically want 100% renewable energy + cold-weather free cooling</li>
        <li>&bull; You need AMD EPYC Milan compute on 10Gbps (V3 KVM Slices) at budget pricing</li>
        <li>&bull; You prefer dedicated physical CPU cores via NVMe VDS at $8/mo entry</li>
        <li>&bull; You pay from a CNY wallet via Alipay and need North American compute</li>
        <li>&bull; You require a published progressive SLA with 100% refund at &lt;95% uptime</li>
        <li>&bull; You want a wholly original Canadian operator (not a US-headquartered reseller)</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4"><strong>Skip ServaRica if:</strong></p>
      <ul class="text-gray-300 text-sm space-y-2">
        <li>&bull; You need multiple geographic regions &mdash; <a href="https://my.racknerd.com/aff.php?aff=16299" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">RackNerd's 8 DCs</a> or <a href="https://my.racknerd.com/aff.php?aff=16299" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">Vultr's 32+ DCs</a> cover what ServaRica doesn't</li>
        <li>&bull; You want the absolute cheapest VPS &mdash; <a href="https://my.racknerd.com/aff.php?aff=16299" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">RackNerd's $10.98/yr annual floor</a> wins</li>
        <li>&bull; You need GPU instances &mdash; ServaRica does not offer GPU compute</li>
        <li>&bull; You run public proxy servers, IRC servers, gambling sites, or TOR exit nodes (against ToS)</li>
        <li>&bull; You require unmetered 1Gbps bandwidth on the storage tier (4TB cap applies)</li>
        <li>&bull; You need <5 minute provisioning &mdash; ServaRica orders may take 'several hours' if fraud-screening triggers</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <!-- H2-9 (Final Verdict - 9th section) -->
    <div class="bg-[#111] border border-white/5 rounded-2xl p-8 mb-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">H2-9: The Final Verdict</h2>
      <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">ServaRica is a focused pick for a specific buyer profile: developers who want Canadian-jurisdiction VPS outside US scope, AMD EPYC Milan compute on 10Gbps with dedicated cores, 100% renewable energy from Hydro-Quebec, and an unusual Alipay + crypto payment stack. The 9-person engineering team, 16-year operating history, and wholly-original operator story (Rica Web Services Inc.) are not the kind of operational detail you find at every budget VPS host.</p>
      <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">For the broader &ldquo;cheapest VPS available&rdquo; question, <a href="https://my.racknerd.com/aff.php?aff=16299" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">RackNerd's annual-special floor at $10.98/yr across 8 datacenters</a> still wins on price-per-month. For APAC-heavy workloads, <a href="https://my.racknerd.com/aff.php?aff=16299" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">LightNode's 40+ locations from $7.70/mo</a> or Vultr's 32+ global DCs are the right pick. But for the niche ServaRica actually targets &mdash; Canadian jurisdiction + EPYC + 10Gbps + renewable + Alipay/crypto on a North American provider &mdash; it's the only operator offering this combination in 2026.</p>
